  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q$ still contains ${x}^{7}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then, $\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}=\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{x}^{7}}=2x,$ which is a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true.",
